  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1140 AM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nDry and unseasonably warm autumn weather will persist through the\nweek ahead. For tonight, mostly clear skies will prevail with a\ndry airmass and upper level ridging in place. There is still some\nlow level moisture over southeast Colorado, combined with\nradiational cooling should support patchy/areas of fog from Limon\nsouthward.  \n\nSaturday will be warm and dry, but a weak dry cool front will push\nacross the plains during the day. Despite this front, we'll see a\nwell mixed airmass and thus temperatures climbing well above \nseasonal normals and into the lower to mid 80s over most of the \nplains. Northerly breezes gusting to around 20-25 mph will bring \nelevated fire weather conditions to the plains east of I-25. For \nSunday, temperatures will be just a tad cooler in the post- \nfrontal airmass. An isolated shower/weak storm will be possible in\nthe mountains mainly south of I-70, with just enough moisture \nbuilding under the ridge. \n\nMonday will likely feature the warmest day of October, with\nexcellent model agreement that we'll reach the mid to upper 80s\nacross the plains and I-25 Corridor. That pushes us near record\nhighs for the date. The record high for both Denver and Fort \nCollins is 87F set in 2024 so we should be very close to that, but\nBoulder's record is 90F set in 1947 and doubt we would touch that\ngiven thermal profiles and only light downslope flow. Once again,\nwe can't rule out a late day shower over the high country given \nsuch strong heating and a little moisture getting trapped under \nthe ridge. \n\nA weak disturbance is still on track to push across the state by\nTuesday, which will bring a chance of showers and a weak storm or\ntwo to the mountains. With a little forcing to work with, I \nwouldn't be surprised to see a couple sneak onto the adjacent \nplains before ending Tuesday night. \n\nModels maintain excellent agreement on the synoptic pattern with\ngenerally flat upper level ridging and light downslope flow into \nlate next week. That would spell a continuation of much above \nnormal temperatures and more dry weather.",
